  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday through next Friday)\nIssued at 1121 PM CDT FRI OCT 2 2026\n\nIn general, the overall thinking and synoptic setup with the\nforecast through the weekend remains unchanged. Our two main\nfeatures remain the stalled front to the north, and the\ndisturbance moving inland from the Gulf. Even 36-hours out, there\nare still question marks as to the strength of the Gulf\ndisturbance, and the position of the stalled front. At this time,\nit still looks like the front has stalled north of where it was\npreviously expected to. On the other side of the coin however, it\nlooks like this disturbance will move inland slightly more\norganized than previously anticipated. Guidance has also not done\na good job capturing the scope of diurnally/outflow driven\nshowers and storms given these high PWAT values. Because of that,\neven though rainfall amounts may be slightly overdone with the\nmain show on Sunday, some of the rainfall on Friday and Saturday\nmay help make up for this deficit.\n\nThe bulk of the rainfall still looks to fall on Sunday, as the\ncold front starts to drop south through the region. At this time,\nthe highest rainfall amounts look to fall south of I-20, with the\nhighest amounts falling closer to I-85 near the apex of this Gulf\ndisturbance. Rainfall amounts between I-20 and I-85 will\ngenerally be between 1-2 inches, with areas along and south of I-\n85 pushing 2-3 inches. It's also worth noting that the NAM suite\nof models are consistently highlighting locally heavy pockets of\n5+ inches, but the only thing consistent here are the values. The\nlocation of these amounts varies from run to run, with the only\nconsistent thing being the amounts. Given the aforementioned high\nPWAT values, local amounts like this are certainly not out of the\nquestion.\n\nBy Tuesday morning, a deep trough moving through the Midwest will\nhave pushed the stalled cold front through the region. This will\nusher in much more seasonable temperatures through next week,\nwith high temperatures ranging in the upper-70s and low-80s.\n\n/44/",
